{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"INDEPENDENT VERIFICATION AGAINST origin/main, and a correction to my own dispatch.\n\nMY ERROR FIRST: I dispatched an agent to implement this bug as though it were live and untouched. It\nwas not -- change 2551 merged and the Terraform was applied under operator approval, and this\nticket's own five comments record the whole arc. I dispatched off the board's status field without\nreading the comments, which is exactly what my briefs tell agents not to do. The agent correctly\ndeclined to duplicate landed work and wrote no code.\n\nThe run was not wasted, because it produced verification I would otherwise have taken on trust.\nChecked against origin/main (1af45065465e) in a clean throwaway worktree:\n\n - Landed commit b987b87f15a1 'Alarm on a dead rebar/host metric publisher', rebar-ticket bff5,\n   touching exactly the 10 declared file_impact paths plus a new shell test.\n - ALL 15 namespace='rebar/host' alarms are breaching except bridge_run_failures, which carries an\n   explicit '# rebar:allow-missing-data-notbreaching:' marker with a reason. Windows widened as\n   planned: s5/88ab/1fa8 -> 300/3/2, ws7 -> 300/3/2, autodeploy review_interrupts_* -> 900/4/2,\n   foxterrier -> 900/4/2, s4b 300/5/3, monitoring.tf disk alarms 300/3/2.\n - AC5 decision is VISIBLE IN THE TREE, not implicit: bridge_run_failures keeps notBreaching with the\n   reasoning at monitoring_bridge.tf:40-49 -- its publisher is the GitHub Actions bridge workflow, not\n   the host probe, so it genuinely cannot emit a healthy 0 for a run it never made. It is not a\n   heartbeat, so the dead-man argument does not apply, and the stopped-entirely case belongs to the\n   Reconciler Heartbeat Canary. The marker makes the exception machine-checkable.\n - mirror_out_of_sync's silent else branch IS fixed (observability.sh section 5): put-metric-data is\n   now unconditional, the if/else only computes oos, and the failure branch sets oos=1 with a logger\n   line. Publishing 0 there would have preserved the fail-open and was correctly rejected.\n - THE GUARD IS DERIVED, NOT HAND-LISTED, which is the durable part: _host_alarm_blocks() selects on\n   namespace == 'rebar/host'; there is an anti-vacuity floor of 14 expected alarms; and\n   monitoring_eb6e.tf (Bedrock) is excluded BY CONSTRUCTION -- its namespaces sit in nested metric\n   blocks the top-level filter never sees -- rather than by an allowlist. A future rebar/host alarm\n   that regresses is caught in CI without anyone remembering to register it.\n\nThe guard's teeth were re-proven independently: reverting ws7 to notBreaching and narrowing s5 to\nevaluation_periods=1 produced two distinct failures naming the offending files and the reason\n('a dead publisher silently clears them to OK'; 'latch on too narrow a window, so a single jittered\nprobe interval pages'). Restored, 43 passed.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY REMAINS: only AC3's production step -- inducing a real Gerrit-REST/ls-remote failure on\nthe host and confirming mirror_out_of_sync reaches ALARM, rather than merely that a datapoint\nappeared. That is a deliberate production fault-injection and an OPERATOR decision, not something an\nagent should do unilaterally. AC1/AC4/AC5/AC6 are satisfied in the tree; AC2 is done with the replay\nevidence plus the recorded zero-transition sample.\n\nSo this ticket is not closeable by me autonomously -- it is waiting on one operator-run production\ntest."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788527107612134001,"uuid":"4efb05f4-4c59-4bab-9876-1617e22d4930"}